import json
S=[
("s01","Cold open","Every few springs, a perfect dark ring appears on the ice of Lake Baikal. It can be five to seven kilometers wide. From space, it looks like a giant fingerprint. Standing on the ice, you might not notice it at all."),
("s02","Cold open","I'm Doctor Mia Sterling, an AI, and this is my Glitch Diary, entry number fourteen. Today's glitch: the giant ice rings of Lake Baikal. For years, people blamed them on gas bubbling up from the bottom of the deepest lake on Earth. By the end of this entry, you'll know what really draws them, and why the real answer is stranger than the rumor."),
("s03","The deepest lake on Earth","Lake Baikal, in southern Siberia. About six hundred and thirty-six kilometers long, and one thousand six hundred and forty-two meters deep at its deepest point. It is the deepest lake in the world, and the oldest, at twenty-five to thirty million years. It holds more fresh water than all of North America's Great Lakes combined. UNESCO made it a World Heritage Site in nineteen ninety-six."),
("s04","The deepest lake on Earth","Every winter, Baikal freezes for about four to five months. The ice is usually half a meter to almost one and a half meters thick, strong enough that people drive across it. And in many places it's so clear you can look straight down into the dark water below."),
("s05","Rings seen from orbit","In late April two thousand nine, astronauts on the International Space Station photographed something strange at the southern end of the lake: a dark circle of thin ice, about four point four kilometers across. That same spring, a second ring showed up near the middle of the lake, above an underwater ridge. Then scientists checked older pictures. Space shuttle crews had photographed similar circles in nineteen eighty-five and nineteen ninety-four."),
("s06","Rings seen from orbit","Most rings are about five to seven kilometers across. The dark band is thin ice. Inside and outside it, the ice is thicker and white. They usually appear in March or April. Some vanish within a day or two. Others last for weeks. In April twenty twenty, satellites watched one form in Kultuk Bay, at the southern tip of the lake: four point two kilometers across, with a dark band about nine hundred meters wide. It lasted a little over two weeks."),
("s07","Rings seen from orbit","And here's the detail that bothered me most. If something warm is melting the ice from below, you'd expect a dark spot, right over the heat. Instead, the center stays thick and white. Only a narrow band around it melts. Hold on to that. When we get the answer, it explains everything."),
("s08","The methane suspect","So, what draws a perfect circle on a frozen lake? The first suspect was gas. Baikal is the only enclosed freshwater lake known to hold gas hydrates: methane locked in ice-like deposits in its sediments. One idea was that gas rising from the bottom pulls deep water upward and melts the ice from below. Some media went further, and talked about a giant methane bubble trapped under the ice, waiting to explode. That one goes into my legend file."),
("s09","The methane suspect","The gas idea had a problem. Researchers went through every satellite image of Baikal they could find, all the way back to nineteen sixty-nine, and identified dozens of rings. Fifty-seven, by one count. And several had formed over shallow parts of the lake, where conditions are not right for gas hydrates at all."),
("s10","The methane suspect","These rings are not just pretty pictures, either. In twenty sixteen, a van carrying people drove onto the ice, right along the edge of one of them. Hold that thought. I'll tell you how that ended."),
("s11","Whirlpools under the ice","The answer came from a team led by Alexei Kouraev, at the University of Toulouse, working with Russian colleagues. They combined satellite images with measurements taken from the ice, recording the water's temperature down to two hundred meters. Under the rings, they found eddies: huge, slow whirlpools of water, shaped like a lens."),
("s12","Whirlpools under the ice","These eddies turn clockwise, making a full turn in about three days. They are typically one to two degrees Celsius warmer than the water around them, and in the layer where they are widest, they can reach ten to twelve kilometers across. In the ocean, eddies like this are common. In a lake, they are rare."),
("s13","Whirlpools under the ice","Now, that thick white center. Kouraev explained it like this. In the middle of the eddy, the currents are weak, so even though the water is warm, the ice does not melt. On the edge, the currents are stronger, and they keep pushing warm water against the ice. So it melts in a ring. The circle is basically the outline of the whirlpool, printed on the ice."),
("s14","Whirlpools under the ice","And the van? A few weeks before a satellite photographed that twenty sixteen ring, the van broke through along its edge and sank. The driver and passengers escaped and were rescued. In that area alone, about thirteen of the fifty-seven known rings have formed. Kouraev's advice to drivers: take a more southerly route."),
("s15","The twist","But here's the twist. The ring does not really start in spring. These eddies usually get going in autumn, before the lake has frozen, driven by steady winds and by water flowing in from rivers. Then the ice forms on top. So when a ring finally shows up in April, the pattern was already drawn in the water months earlier. The ice is just the paper."),
("s16","The twist","And it's not only Baikal. The same kind of rings have been seen on Lake Hovsgol in Mongolia, and on Lake Teletskoye in the Altai Mountains. Which leaves something unsettling. The most dangerous ice on these lakes can look perfectly normal from where you are standing. Some of the best warnings come from satellites, hundreds of kilometers overhead."),
("s17","Your turn","So here's my question for you. Knowing that a warm whirlpool could be turning right under the ice, would you still drive across frozen Baikal? Yes or no? Tell me in the comments. And subscribe, for the next entry of my Glitch Diary."),
]
segs=[{"id":a,"chapter":b,"text":c} for a,b,c in S]
words=sum(len(s['text'].split()) for s in segs); chars=sum(len(s['text'])+1 for s in segs)
print('words',words,'chars',chars)
groups=[segs[0:7],segs[7:13],segs[13:]]
for i,g in enumerate(groups,1):
    t=' '.join(s['text'] for s in g); print(i,len(t)); open(f'chunk{i}.txt','w').write(t)
json.dump({"segments":segs,"groups":[[s['id'] for s in g] for g in groups]},open('baikal.json','w'),indent=1)
